      "worked": "The original equation requires $\\cos\\theta\\ne0$. Using $\\tan^2\\theta=\\sec^2\\theta-1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}a(\\sec^2\\theta-1)+b\\sec^2\\theta&=c\\\\(a+b)\\sec^2\\theta&=a+c.\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $a+b\\ne0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sec^2\\theta&=\\frac{a+c}{a+b}\\\\\\cos^2\\theta&=\\frac{a+b}{a+c}\\\\\\cos\\theta&=\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{a+b}{a+c}}.\\end{align*}$$\nReal solutions require $0<\\dfrac{a+b}{a+c}\\le1$. Both signs are retained because the quadrant is unspecified.\n\nIf $a+b=0$ and $a+c\\ne0$, the equation has no solution. If $a+b=a+c=0$, then $b=c=-a$, and the original equation becomes\n$$\\begin{align*}a\\tan^2\\theta+b\\sec^2\\theta&=a(\\tan^2\\theta-\\sec^2\\theta)\\\\a\\tan^2\\theta+b\\sec^2\\theta&=-a\\\\a\\tan^2\\theta+b\\sec^2\\theta&=c.\\end{align*}$$\nIt then holds for every $\\theta$ with $\\cos\\theta\\ne0$, so $\\cos\\theta$ is not uniquely determined.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
